{
 "thread": "IHvKaCaNRVI",
 "subject": "Relative color scale in ggplot",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2014-07-02T20:17:15Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "o-n5LdxnVPoJ",
   "from": "Michael",
   "author": "6e4e37c602c2b104",
   "date": "2014-07-02T18:26:41Z",
   "subject": "Relative color scale in ggplot",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi all,<div><br></div><div>I have a ggplot where colors scale by default to the global min and max of a variable. I&#39;d like to scale the color to the max response within a particular x value. An example may be clearer:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><div><div><font color=\"#660066\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">set.seed(1)</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(rep(&quot;a&quot;, 10), rep(&quot;b&quot;, 10)),</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">                 y=c(sample(1:4, 10, replace=T), sample(1:6, 10, replace=T)))</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">ggplot(data=df, aes(x=x, y=y)) + </font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">  stat_sum(aes(group=x, color=y)) +</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">  scale_size_area(max_size=15) +</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">  scale_color_gradient(low=&quot;red&quot;, high=&quot;green&quot;)</font></div></div></div></code></div><br>That produces the following.</div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-CAfwjjpwnGU/U7ROmBMDj2I/AAAAAAAAI70/P8mGpe7BXio/s1600/Rplot.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-CAfwjjpwnGU/U7ROmBMDj2I/AAAAAAAAI70/P8mGpe7BXio/s1600/Rplot.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792468804348000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0ptyFqYBortMKC2sduoZ7z\"><img src=\"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-CAfwjjpwnGU/U7ROmBMDj2I/AAAAAAAAI70/P8mGpe7BXio/s1600/Rplot.png\" border=\"0\"></a></p><div style=\"text-align:left\"><br></div><div><br></div><div>What I&#39;d like is for the point at (a, 4) to be the same color as the one at (b, 6). There&#39;s probably a simple solution, but I&#39;m stuck. Thanks in advance,</div><div><br></div><div>Michael</div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "A1YYFnsdiTIJ",
   "from": "Noam Ross",
   "author": "8c8f0ca54c4a2459",
   "date": "2014-07-02T18:47:18Z",
   "subject": "Re: [D-RUG]: Relative color scale in ggplot",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:1em;font-family:Helvetica,arial,freesans,clean,sans-serif;color:rgb(34,34,34);border:none;line-height:1.2;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><p style=\"margin:1em 0px\">You can normalize the <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:nowrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248)\">y</code> values within the <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:nowrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248)\">x</code> groups and use these to assign colors.  The numbers in the legend become meaningless, though, and you’ll want to modify or get rid of it:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;overflow:auto;margin:1em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;white-space:pre;border-top-left-radius:3px;border-top-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-right-radius:3px;border-bottom-left-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:rgb(248,248,255)\">require(ggplot2)\r\nrequire(plyr)\r\nset.seed(1)\r\ndf &lt;- data.frame(x=c(rep(&quot;a&quot;, 10), rep(&quot;b&quot;, 10)),\r\n                 y=c(sample(1:4, 10, replace=T), sample(1:6, 10, replace=T)))\r\ndf &lt;- ddply(df, .(x), transform, y2=(y-min(y))/(max(y)-min(y)))  # &lt;--- Normalized y values are &quot;y2&quot;\r\nggplot(data=df, aes(x=x, y=y)) + \r\n  stat_sum(aes(group=x, color=y2)) +        # &lt;---  Assign color to &quot;y2&quot;\r\n  scale_size_area(max_size=15) +\r\n  scale_color_gradient(low=&quot;red&quot;, high=&quot;green&quot;, breaks=c(0,1),  # &lt;--- Modify the scale labels\r\n                       label=c(&quot;min.group&quot;, &quot;max.group&quot;))\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1em 0px\">You get:</p><p style=\"margin:1em 0px\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/davis-rug/attach/32891d7b16185603/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"Inline image 1\" width=\"562\" height=\"405.9326788218794\"><br></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:1em;font-family:Helvetica,arial,freesans,clean,sans-serif;color:rgb(34,34,34);border:none;line-height:1.2;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><p style=\"margin:1em 0px\"><br></p><p style=\"margin:1em 0px\">\r\nOn Wed, Jul 2, 2014 at 11:26 AM, Michael &lt;<a href style=\"color:rgb(51,51,238)\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mle...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1em 0px\"></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:1em;font-family:Helvetica,arial,freesans,clean,sans-serif;color:rgb(34,34,34);border:none;line-height:1.2;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\nHi all,<div><br></div><div>I have a ggplot where colors scale by default to the global min and max of a variable. I&#39;d like to scale the color to the max response within a particular x value. An example may be clearer:</div>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><div><div><font color=\"#660066\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">set.seed(1)</font></div>\r\n\r\n<div><font color=\"#660066\">df &lt;- data.frame(x=c(rep(&quot;a&quot;, 10), rep(&quot;b&quot;, 10)),</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">                 y=c(sample(1:4, 10, replace=T), sample(1:6, 10, replace=T)))</font></div>\r\n\r\n<div><font color=\"#660066\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">ggplot(data=df, aes(x=x, y=y)) + </font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">  stat_sum(aes(group=x, color=y)) +</font></div><div><font color=\"#660066\">  scale_size_area(max_size=15) +</font></div>\r\n\r\n<div><font color=\"#660066\">  scale_color_gradient(low=&quot;red&quot;, high=&quot;green&quot;)</font></div></div></div></code></div><br>That produces the following.</div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\">\r\n\r\n<a href=\"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-CAfwjjpwnGU/U7ROmBMDj2I/AAAAAAAAI70/P8mGpe7BXio/s1600/Rplot.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-CAfwjjpwnGU/U7ROmBMDj2I/AAAAAAAAI70/P8mGpe7BXio/s1600/Rplot.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792468804351000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1qKVv-gy2IQxi35raHFxqj\"><img src=\"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-CAfwjjpwnGU/U7ROmBMDj2I/AAAAAAAAI70/P8mGpe7BXio/s1600/Rplot.png\" border=\"0\"></a></p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"text-align:left\"><br></div><div><br></div><div>What I&#39;d like is for the point at (a, 4) to be the same color as the one at (b, 6). There&#39;s probably a simple solution, but I&#39;m stuck. Thanks in advance,</div>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div>Michael</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><div><br></div></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
